For the spin model, define the unscaled energy score by $$\mathcal E_N(\sigma)
 =\sum_{k=1}^{M_N}J_k\prod_{\ell=1}^p\sigma_{i_{\ell,k}}
   +\sum_{i=1}^N H_i\sigma_i.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
Suppose a leaf partition function is iterated by power means, with exponents $m_1,\ldots,m_{L-1}$, and $K_1,\ldots,K_{L-1}$ are its tilted kernels. Let $K_L$ be its terminal Gibbs kernel. Multiplying each spin weight by $A$ changes the iterated root logarithm by $\log Y_0$, where <a href="#tree:Y-recursion" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tree:Y-recursion">[tree:Y-recursion]</a> uses the kernels before insertion. The new kernels are <a href="#tree:updated-kernel" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="tree:updated-kernel">[tree:updated-kernel]</a>.*


*For an internal vertex $\beta$ of a prescribed tree, let $d_\beta$ be its depth, let $n_\beta$ be its number of children, and put $$\gamma_\beta=m_{d_\beta}-n_\beta m_{d_\beta+1}.$$ The conditional joint density of its updated tree law is $$\label{tree:density}
  W_{\mathcal T}(A)
  :=\frac{dP_{\mathcal T}^A}{dP_{\mathcal T}}
  =\prod_{a\in\operatorname{Leaves}(\mathcal T)}A^a
       \prod_{\beta\in\operatorname{Int}(\mathcal T)}
            Y_\beta^{\gamma_\beta}.$$ Here $Y_\beta$ is $Y_{d_\beta}$ evaluated on the prefix at $\beta$.*

*The recursion is monotone and homogeneous: if $a\leq A\leq b$, for positive root-measurable $a,b$, then $a\leq Y_d\leq b$ at every depth; and multiplying $A$ by a positive root-measurable constant multiplies every $Y_d$ by that constant.

For a bounded real path function $D$ and $A=1+tD$, $$\label{tree:log-series}
  \log Y_0
   =\sum_{k\geq1}\frac{(-1)^{k-1}t^k}{k}
       \mathbb E_{Q_k}E_{\mathcal T}
          \prod_{a=1}^k D^a,
       \qquad |t|\|D\|_\infty<1.$$ This identity is conditional on the root. If $|t|\|D\|_\infty\leq c<1$, the absolute value of the remainder after order $K$ is at most $$\label{tree:log-tail}
  \sum_{k>K}\frac{c^k}{k}.$$

Let $H$ be a bounded function of the variables on the old tree, fixed as the insertion parameters vary. For $A_{\boldsymbol t}=1+\sum_{j=1}^k t_jD_j$, with bounded real path functions $D_j$, one has $$\label{tree:mixed-expansion}
  \left.\partial_{t_1}\cdots\partial_{t_k}
       E_{\mathcal T}^{A_{\boldsymbol t}}
        \frac{H}{A_{\boldsymbol t}^{a_*}}
  \right|_{\boldsymbol t=0}
   =\sum_E c_E\,
       E_{\mathcal T_E}
          \left[H\prod_{j=1}^kD_j^{a_j(E)}\right].$$ The formula holds in any fixed order of the colors. For identical directions $D_j=D$, the coefficient of $t^k$ is the right-hand side divided by $k!$. The analytic bounds established above apply to its left-hand side, uniformly over the path kernels. The empty extension has weight $1$.

Suppose the next color must follow a specified reference prefix to depth $d$ and then enter a child distinct from all $j\geq1$ children there that contain reference paths. Apart from these ancestry constraints its continuation is unrestricted. The sum of the coefficients of all allowed choices is $$\label{tree:one-reference-total}
  m_d-jm_{d+1}.$$ Consequently, if the anchor and the colors must have a prescribed tree $S$, the total coefficient over all compatible extensions of any old tree is $$\label{tree:K}
 \kappa(S)=\prod_{v\in\operatorname{Br}(S)}
           \prod_{j=1}^{a_v-1}(m_{d_v}-jm_{d_v+1}),$$ where $a_v$ and $d_v$ are the child count and depth of each branching vertex in the prescribed reference tree. More generally, the same one-step rule can be iterated starting from any already constructed set of references.

Assume $m_d=d/L$ and put $\delta=L^{-1}$. Start with an old tree having $q$ leaves and assign $k$ colors by the canceled-anchor extension rule. Prescribe the reference shape of the anchor and the colors, possibly with additional restrictions on allowed extensions. Suppose $b$ specified branching vertices of that shape have depths which are not branching depths anywhere in the old tree. Then $$\label{tree:charging-bound}
   \sum_{E\text{ allowed}}|c_E|\leq C_{q,k}\delta^b.$$ The constant is independent of $L$, the prescribed depths, and their coincidences at different vertices.*

*In particular, for these $b$ vertices let $$J=\prod_v\prod_{j=1}^{a_v-1}(m_{d_v}-jm_{d_v+1}).$$ If $m_{d_v}\geq\eta>0$ at all of them, then $J\ne0$ and $$\label{tree:relative-charging}
   \frac{1}{|J|}\sum_{E\text{ allowed}}|c_E|
        \leq C_{q,k,\eta}.$$

For every fixed $k$ there is $C_k<\infty$, independent of $L$ and $\eta$, such that $$\label{tree:irregular-probability}
  Q_k\{\mathcal T\text{ is not regular}\}
        \leq C_k(\eta+L^{-1}).$$ For any particular labeled topology with $a$ branching vertices and any consistent assignment of their depths, $$\label{tree:assignment-probability}
  Q_k\{\text{that topology and depth assignment}\}
       \leq C_kL^{-a}.$$ Deleting leaves from a regular tree produces a regular tree, after discarding branching vertices that become unary.

The increments $\Delta_N^{L,\mathbf u}=P_{N+1}^{L,\mathbf u}-P_N^{L,\mathbf u}$ are uniformly bounded in $N,L,\mathbf u$. There is a reservoir on $N$ spins with physical interaction count of mean $$\label{cavity:reservoir-mean}
 \rho_N=\alpha(N+1)\left(\frac{N}{N+1}\right)^p,$$ the usual fields, and the perturbation counts of means $c_\nu s_N$, such that $$\label{cavity:identity}
 \Delta_N^{L,\mathbf u}
 =\log2+\mathbb E\log Y_{0,\rm s}-\mathbb E\log Y_{0,\rm b}+o_N(1).$$ The error is uniform in $L,\mathbf u$. The insertion recursion uses the tilted reservoir kernels and independent new root disorder, with terminal insertions $$\begin{aligned}
 A_{\rm s}(\sigma)&=\frac12\sum_{\varepsilon=\pm1}
 \exp\left(h\varepsilon+\sum_{j=1}^{K_{\rm s}}
 \theta_j(\sigma_{i_{1,j}},\ldots,\sigma_{i_{p-1,j}},\varepsilon)\right),
 \label{cavity:site-insertion}\\
 A_{\rm b}(\sigma)&=\exp\left(\sum_{j=1}^{K_{\rm b}}
 \theta_j(\sigma_{i_{1,j}},\ldots,\sigma_{i_{p,j}})\right),
 \label{cavity:bond-insertion}
\end{aligned}$$ where $K_{\rm s}\sim\mathop{\mathrm{Poisson}}(\alpha p)$, $K_{\rm b}\sim\mathop{\mathrm{Poisson}}(\alpha(p-1))$, and all displayed indices are independent uniform indices in $[N]$.

For every fixed labeled topology $S$ and every $\eta>0$, $$\lim_{L\to\infty}\limsup_{N\to\infty}
       \mathcal A_{L,\eta}v_N(S,d)=0,
 \label{decor:conditional-concentration-equation}$$ where the average ranges over all branching depths of $S$ and an additional regular depth $d$ strictly before its first split. For a singleton, the average ranges over $d$ alone.

For the trial laws of <a href="#comp:encoding" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="comp:encoding">22</a>, with $\mathbf m^{(L)}=(1/L,\ldots,(L-1)/L)$, $$\label{comp:increment-comparison}
 \lim_{L\to\infty}\limsup_{N\to\infty}
 \left|\Delta_N^{L,\mathbf u_{L,N}}
 -\mathcal B_{L-1}(\zeta_{L,N},\mathbf m^{(L)})\right|=0.$$

Couple two interaction laws $\theta,\theta'$ and two field laws $h,h'$, each satisfying <a href="#model:integrability" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="model:integrability">[model:integrability]</a> for the fixed $p$ and $\alpha$, and put $D=\mathbb E\max_s|\theta(s)-\theta'(s)|$ and $d=\mathbb E|h-h'|$. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 |F_N-F_N'|&\le\alpha D+d,\label{reduction:free-stability}\\
 |\mathcal B_r(\zeta,\mathbf m)-\mathcal B_r'(\zeta,\mathbf m)|
 &\le\alpha(2p-1)D+d\label{reduction:trial-stability}
\end{aligned}$$ uniformly in $N,r$ and all trial laws, including $r=0$. If $c$ is an integrable random constant associated with an interaction and $\widehat\theta=\theta-c$, then $$\label{reduction:shift}
 \widehat F_N=F_N-\alpha\mathbb Ec,
 \qquad\widehat{\mathcal B}_r=\mathcal B_r-\alpha\mathbb Ec.$$

For each model in [models:spin,models:sat], the limit $$g=\lim_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\mathbb E\max_\sigma\mathcal E_N(\sigma)$$ exists and satisfies $$\label{models:ground-formula}
 g=\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\frac1\beta
       \inf_{r\ge0}\inf_{\zeta,\mathbf m}
          \mathcal B_{r,\beta}(\zeta,\mathbf m).$$ More precisely, the expression inside the temperature limit lies in $[g,g+\log(2)/\beta]$. For unit-weight SAT, the limiting expected minimum number of violated clauses per variable is $-g$.
